      what: 'After his temple sermon Jeremiah is nearly killed but is spared. He wears a wooden yoke to show that the nations must serve Babylon; the false prophet Hananiah breaks it and promises a quick return, and dies within the year. Jeremiah writes to the exiles in Babylon: build houses, plant gardens, "seek the welfare of the city," for after seventy years God will bring them back: "I know the plans I have for you."\n\nChapters 30–33, the "Book of Comfort," look beyond judgment. God promises to restore Israel and Judah and make "a new covenant," writing His law on their hearts and forgiving their sin. While Jerusalem is under siege, Jeremiah buys a field at Anathoth as a sign that houses and fields will again be bought in the land. God promises a righteous Branch from David. The section ends with the faithful Rechabites as a rebuke to Judah.',
